course: dictionary
lesson: 8144
title: Words that show feelings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you how someone feels or how they speak, even when the sentence does not say it outright. Look at the words around the word to find the clue. In this story, the home calls Toad «sorrowfully, reproachfully», and it gives a «plaintive reminder» that it wants him back. Plaintive means sad-sounding, so the mood is sad, not cross.
explain: Reproachfully means in a way that shows you are disappointed with someone, or that you blame them. Think of a parent who looks at you after you break a promise. They may not shout, but their look says they expected better from you.
explain: The example also says «no bitterness or anger». That is a clue too. Reproachfully does not have to mean angry. It can be quiet and sad, with a hint that someone has let you down.
fact: Reproachfully is an adverb: it tells you how something is done, such as how someone looks, speaks or sighs.
fact: «Reproachfully» means in a way that shows you are disappointed with someone or blame them.
fact: Reproachfully can be quiet and sad, without shouting or anger.
fact: «Plaintive» means sad-sounding, so a plaintive voice sounds sorrowful.
fact: In The Wind in the Willows, the Rat speaks reproachfully to Toad, showing quiet disapproval.
exercise: «She looked at him reproachfully when he broke the vase.» What does reproachfully show? | She was lost in thought | *Disappointment or blame | She was fast asleep | Great joy and excitement
exercise: «He sighed reproachfully and shook his head.» Which word tells you how he sighed? | Shook | He | *Reproachfully | Head
exercise: «The Rat spoke reproachfully to Toad, but he did not shout.» What does reproachfully mean here? | Sleepily and slowly | Happily and loudly | *Sadly and disapprovingly, without shouting | Quickly, as if running
test: «Mum looked at the muddy footprints on the clean floor and sighed reproachfully.» What does reproachfully suggest? | Mum is dancing around the room | *Mum is disappointed and blames someone | Mum has lost her keys | Mum is pleased with the mess
test: In «a plaintive reminder», what does plaintive mean? | Happy and bright | Angry and loud | *Sad-sounding | Very quick
exam: Which answer best explains reproachfully? | *A way of showing you are disappointed in someone or blame them | A way of shouting angrily | A way of laughing at a joke | A way of running fast
